<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matsudaira Naomasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1601">1601</a>–<a href="/years/1666">1666</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matsudaira Naomasa was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuki-hideyasu">Yuki Hideyasu</a>. After holding smaller fiefs, he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a> domain in Izumo in 1638 with about 186,000 koku. His Echizen Matsudaira line ruled Matsue from its lakeside castle until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p></div>
